WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.609
Midsommar is a movie about a relationship
going up in flames - literally.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:10.850
Writer-director Ari Aster has described it
as more of a breakup movie than a horror film,

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.680
one that he hopes will leave people, quote,
"feeling a little bit confused about how to

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:15.680
feel."

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.280
So: what happened?

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.600
As Aster has explained in many interviews,
Midsommar is about a relationship coming to

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:23.619
an end.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:24.830
He told USA Today,

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.980
"The goal was ultimately to make a big, operatic
breakup movie that feels the way breakups

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.150
actually feel: catastrophic, like the world
is ending."

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:41.370
"It's an operatic breakup movie and a dark
contemporary fairy tale."

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.170
Like a lot of great horror movies, you're
not really supposed to take everything that

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.510
happens in Midsommar literally.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.490
Whether all of this weird stuff is actually
happening isn't nearly as important as how

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.990
the characters react to it - particularly
Dani.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:00.100
Altered states and hallucinations play a big
role in Midsommar's story, and subjectivity

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.350
is important to how the plot unfolds.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:07.930
From Dani's perspective, the story plays out
as a series of increasingly-shocking revelations.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:13.040
The movie looks a lot different from the perspective
of Pelle , the member of the Hårga commune.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.890
Out of all of Christian's friends who come
to the Hårga festival, he's the only one

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.300
who knows what's going to happen.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.530
“That’s why you look so guilty right now,
because you know.”

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:25.000
The audience also gets competing perspectives
of Dani and Christian's relationship.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.760
The audience sees Dani going through an incredibly
traumatic time, needing the support of a loved

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:30.870
one more than ever.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:35.420
But we also get a look at Christian's perspective,
seeing a guy who's falling out of love with

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:40.850
his girlfriend and tired of dealing with her
drama, whether any of it is her fault or not.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:45.170
Complicating matters are Christian's friends,
most of whom clearly don't like Dani, and

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.080
are encouraging Christian to pull away.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.160
"You've been wanting out of this stupid relationship
for like a year now."

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.360
Dani spends most of the movie as a passive
protagonist, mostly only reacting to events

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.470
as they happen.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:00.240
She doesn't want to be a needy person, and
bends over backwards to take the blame for

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.020
her own clinginess, always going with the
flow in the hopes of salvaging her relationship.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.900
So she ends up on a trip she didn't plan,
under the influence of hallucinogens she didn't

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.400
intend to take.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.700
She gives and she gives, hoping all the while
that Christian, his friends, or the world

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.870
in general will just give her a break.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.510
Nothing in her life feels in her grasp until
the end of the movie, when she's finally crowned

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.390
the May Queen, and given ultimate power over
the commune.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.610
After spending the whole movie as a doormat
for Christian and his friends, she ends up,

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.600
symbolically at least, in control of their
fates.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:40.160
In Midsommar, flowers represent family, with
an early clue to the symbolism being the floral

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.480
wallpaper in the bedroom of Dani's parents,
seen just before they die.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.880
The metaphor can be extended to plants in
general - after the death of Dani’s family,

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.410
the houseplants in her apartment are all dead.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:55.820
In the beginning of the movie, Dani's world
is dark and frigid, but things begin turning

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.520
around for her on the trip, in a place where
the sun rarely stops shining and flowers grow

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.240
in abundance.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.959
Before Dani even consciously realizes it,
she's being introduced to a new family.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:11.190
The first person to wish Dani a happy birthday
- the first birthday she's celebrating without

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.420
her family - is Pelle.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.470
Dani truly begins being pulled into the cult
on the final day, when she joins in the drug-fueled

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:19.740
dance around the maypole.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.260
For the competition, she's gifted a flowered
wreath.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.920
After she wins and becomes the May Queen,
she's soon seen literally up to her neck in

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:33.590
flowers, all-but-smothered by the Hårgas'
adoration - her brothers and sisters, if she'll

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:34.590
have them.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.610
It's a mutually-beneficial relationship.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:41.550
The commune often needs outside members in
order to avoid inbreeding, and Dani needs

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.769
a new place to belong.

00:03:42.769 --> 00:03:48.480
Her old family's lives were violently ended
- her new family has a violent start.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:55.190
Midsommar's violent yet somehow serene ending
works on one level as classic, Wicker Man-style

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.860
folk horror, but it's also a strong metaphor
for a dissolving relationship.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:04.150
Early on in the movie, Dani, a psychology
student, makes reference to the psychological

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.489
process of a breakup, seeing the signs of
her dissolving relationship in Christian's

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:10.150
distancing behavior.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.239
In that sense, the whole bonkers final sequence
can be read as a ritualistic look at how a

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:17.340
relationship comes to an end.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.580
As the movie begins its third act, both Dani
and Christian alienate and agitate their friends

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.130
with their relationship woes.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:28.970
They become separated, experiencing different
parts of the festival on their own.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:33.960
Christian goes into a trancelike state as
he cheats on Dani with Maja, the commune girl

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.910
who's shown signs of being smitten with him
ever since his group arrived.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:42.250
Since we've seen him be drugged and hexed
to the point where his mind seems blank, there

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:47.990
is a strong sense that he is not doing this
voluntarily, urged on by sheer physical desire

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:49.870
rather than conscious thought.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:54.220
It's the exact story we see in a hanging tapestry
earlier in the movie.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:59.440
Meanwhile, Dani goes dancing on her own, unconcerned
about Christian's whereabouts.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.480
With a little help from the Hårga, the long-suffering
couple is finally coming apart.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.400
Dani starts the movie surrounded by people
who don't appreciate her grief, worried that

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:10.720
they'll abandon her.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.780
The only people who show her kindness seem
to be Pelle and the Hårga.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:19.340
After Dani bears witness to Christian and
Maja together in their ritualistic tryst,

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.400
she begins to emotionally break down.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.970
As she does so, she's surrounded by followers
of the May Queen, all emphatically mimicking

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.150
her every noise and emotion.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:33.699
Despite the obvious pain of the moment, this
sense of true connection and empathy is what

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.160
Dani has spent the movie looking for.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.070
She screams, and everyone around her screams
back.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:42.500
Unlike Christian or his friends, they share
her pain.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:45.880
Ever since her family died, that's all she's
really wanted.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.550
One of the more amusing aspects about Midsommar's
climax is the way the events parallel fellow

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:56.590
folk horror film The Wicker Man - as well
as that movie's certifiably insane, Nicolas

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:58.370
Cage-starring remake.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:03.260
That movie inexplicably involved Cage's character
dressing up in a bear costume to disguise

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.990
himself before eventually just running around
punching people, a fact that's impossible

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:12.960
not to think about when Christian is rendered
paralyzed, and sewn into a bear carcass of

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:13.960
his own.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.310
It's the same bear briefly shown caged up
on the commune grounds earlier in the movie,

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.940
not having a clear purpose until the movie's
bloody end.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:26.400
Considering how much Aster has credited the
original Wicker Man with inspiring Midsommar,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.310
it's not hard to read this detail of the bear
suit as an intentional nod to the remake,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.040
adding a bit of dark absurdist humor to the
movie's fiery climax.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.070
As Christian and the bodies of his friends
are being readied for ritual sacrifice, the

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:45.380
proceedings are watched over by a mysterious
figure, face invisible behind a thick veil.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:50.740
The script for Midsommar names this character
as Víðarr, the god of vengeance.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:54.930
But while this figure obviously plays into
some kind of Hårga ritual, he also serves

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.740
as a metaphor for Dani's emotional state.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:02.040
The man refers to Christian, wrapped up in
the bear carcass, as a, quote, "mighty and

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.949
dreadful beast," whose death will purge the
Hårga society of its, quote, "most unholy

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:08.100
affects."

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:13.210
Even though she may not have ordered it deliberately,
this destructive ritual can be read as a very

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:19.460
personal action on the part of Dani, on whose
orders the commune is now effectively acting.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.180
Whether she ordered it or not, this is an
act of vengeance on her behalf.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:28.980
After four years of dating and a long, slow
withering of her romance with Christian, Dani

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.680
is finally asserting herself, and guiding
the relationship to a permanent end.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.300
After spending the whole movie being passive,
this sequence represents her consciously letting

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:44.000
go, unleashing all of her pent-up frustrations
with Christian and his friends, to her relief,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.729
and their doom.

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:48.819
Seeing her smile could come as a total shock.

00:07:48.819 --> 00:07:52.990
It's cathartic, it's weird, and it doesn't
make a ton of literal sense.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:58.290
But it does make sense emotionally, making
for a haunting final sequence that - as Aster's

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:03.010
said he intended - may leave viewers perplexed
about how they're supposed to feel.

